WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:05.460
- [Mark] On the south corner
coast of Hawaii island,

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:08.460
there is one of the last
Hawaiian fishing villages

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:10.493
in the world, and it's Ho'Okena.

00:00:13.444 --> 00:00:16.277
(water splashing)

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:21.140
They still practice traditional
fishing in the old style,

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:23.290
in a canoe chumming the water the way

00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:25.290
that generations of Hawaiians have done.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:31.313
It is something that needs
to be experienced firsthand.

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:35.041
If we don't continue
to learn these things,

00:00:35.041 --> 00:00:37.283
that knowledge will be lost.

00:00:43.235 --> 00:00:45.818
(upbeat music)

00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:48.960
My name's Mark Noguchi.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:51.760
My friends call me Gooch, and
I'm a chef here in Hawaii.

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:56.510
My belief is in preserving the techniques

00:00:56.510 --> 00:00:57.590
and the ingredients that we have

00:00:57.590 --> 00:00:59.140
in this place that I call home.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:03.570
When we talk about Hawaiian food,

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:05.390
there's before Western contact,

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:08.060
and then there's post Western contact.

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:10.330
One of the biggest misconceptions
about Hawaiian food

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:12.890
is how it's always
presented in this gigantic,

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:16.300
you know, luau with the
emu and the kalua pig,

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:17.820
and then the fire knife dancers,

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:20.040
which, that's not even Hawaiian!

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:21.230
Hawaiians, they eat simply.

00:01:21.230 --> 00:01:24.230
It was largely a very ocean, or Palauea,

00:01:24.230 --> 00:01:25.743
and vegetal based diet.

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:29.880
The reason why I'm going
down to Ho'Okena today

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:32.670
is to spend the day with
one of the Hawaiian families

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:35.210
who have graciously called
their friends and family

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:37.173
to come down and share their practice.

00:01:39.631 --> 00:01:42.700
(upbeat music)

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:44.820
- [Charles] Hawaiian practices
around fishing and planting

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:47.053
and growing is all about sustainability.

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:52.273
We fish with nets, and catch what we need.

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:57.513
Opelu is a mackerel; and it's the mainstay

00:01:57.513 --> 00:01:59.563
of the fishing village here.

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:03.420
If you were to try and go in the water

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:05.320
and catch them with a
spear or something else,

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:06.510
they're gonna just leave.

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:09.153
They're not gonna stay
around, you need to feed them.

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:14.420
You would prepare their bait,
which was 100% vegetable.

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:15.620
We call that the pololu.

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:20.520
That's put into a bag and the
bag is thrown into the ocean,

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:24.900
and when it's yanked, all the
pololu-or the bait-is let go.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:27.183
The fish aggregate and
come around and feed.

00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:31.400
We don't separate ourselves from the land.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:34.273
We don't separate ourselves
from what feeds us.

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:38.680
So, it's important then to
sustain those aggregation areas,

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:40.890
and they did that by feeding the fish

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:43.810
during the off season
without even catching them.

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:46.240
And this was a means to train the fish

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:49.580
to come back to the
same area all the time.

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:51.130
It's almost like ocean farming.

00:02:55.952 --> 00:02:58.535
(upbeat music)

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:02.010
- [Mark] So, after you
harvest all of this fish,

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:04.940
you gotta clean it; and you see aunties,

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:08.820
and you see husbands; and
you see friends come around.

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:10.870
Everybody knows that there's a job to do.

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:13.160
Today, we're gonna prepare opelu

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:14.693
in as many ways as possible.

00:03:19.210 --> 00:03:22.090
Fried, seasoned just with pa'akai,

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:23.890
keeping it super simple.

00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:25.950
We're also gonna have opelu raw,

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:29.270
which is just cut up, and
it's just lightly seasoned.

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:32.060
Opelu lomi, where you
take it and you lomi it,

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:34.130
and you turn it, and you mix
it with other ingredients.

00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:37.080
Onions, tomatoes, green onions,

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:39.513
seasoned with like just
chili pepper and salt.

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:43.530
We also have poi today-fresh poi.

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:45.630
Poi is taro that's been cooked,

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:47.040
and it's been mixed with water,

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:49.423
and it is one of our staple starches.

00:03:50.450 --> 00:03:52.370
We also have ulu, which is breadfruit.

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:55.790
Breadfruit is another traditional
Hawaiian staple as well.

00:03:55.790 --> 00:03:58.463
And this is how we eat, everyday, simple.

00:04:00.409 --> 00:04:04.461
(speaking foreign language)

00:04:04.461 --> 00:04:06.220
One thing that we always
do: we bless the food,

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:08.660
that it's a non-denominational mahalo,

00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:09.643
to all the hands and all the spirits

00:04:09.643 --> 00:04:11.560
and all the people that have come together

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:13.100
to put all this food together.

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:14.545
Mahalo.

00:04:14.545 --> 00:04:17.690
(crowd clapping)

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:18.963
And then you eat.

00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:22.690
You just sit around and hang out.

00:04:22.690 --> 00:04:24.410
That's when the stories come out;

00:04:24.410 --> 00:04:28.140
that's everything that we
worked for for that day.

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:31.330
It reinforces, for myself, the importance

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:33.120
of being able to know the difference

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:35.453
of what it is that we're
cooking and serving.

00:04:36.858 --> 00:04:37.691
So good.

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:40.890
In today's day and age,

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:43.210
where we are all worried
about our resources,

00:04:43.210 --> 00:04:45.280
there are people still doing their part

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:47.623
to preserve an eco system.

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:51.940
It lends hope for us to continue

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:54.253
to preserve the place that we call home.

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:59.030
We need to take these lessons
that aren't shared worldwide;

00:04:59.030 --> 00:05:01.100
we need to be able to take these lessons

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:03.704
and apply them to our daily life.

00:05:03.704 --> 00:05:06.787
(light upbeat music)

